.TH PRIMITIVE 1
.SH NAME
primitive \- command line interface for Primitive email and Functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B primitive
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B primitive
.B --help
.br
.B primitive
.I command
.B --help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B primitive
is the command line interface for Primitive. It sends mail, inspects inbound and outbound email, manages domains and webhook endpoints, deploys Primitive Functions, and exposes generated API operations for scripting.
.PP
Most commands print human-readable output by default. Commands that expose JSON usually provide a
.B --json
flag or use generated operation output that can be piped into tools such as
.BR jq (1).
.PP
With
.BR --json ,
stdout is exactly one JSON document, on success and on failure, and stderr
stays empty, so output merged with 2>&1 still parses. Notices the command
would print on stderr go in the document's
.B warnings
array; a failure adds
.B error
and
.BR exit_code .
Generated API commands keep their usual stdout shape with
.BR --json :
the data payload only. They do not print the
.B next cursor:
line then; add
.B --envelope
to get the full response envelope, with the pagination cursor in
.B meta.cursor
and empty-result hints in
.BR summary .
.B primitive listen
streams JSONL and is not covered by this rule.
.SH LOCAL WEBHOOK EVENTS
.PP
.B primitive listen
receives existing webhook events without a public endpoint. It prints complete
JSONL bodies on stdout and status on stderr. Use saved login credentials or
PRIMITIVE_API_KEY. A private default destination is saved per API host and account.
.PP
.B primitive listen --notify-session UUID --sender person@example.com
queues external email metadata into one exact loaded native Codex session.
Requires an existing private native local-session Unix socket and a connected-agent
credential. This mode requires WebSocket and the shared saved subscription;
--transport poll and a custom --subscription are rejected. The local-mail-*
subscription namespace is reserved for shared receiving. No runtime is launched. Repeat --sender or use comma-separated exact
addresses. Routine authenticated status interactions do not notify the session.
Verified receiver presence controls are answered deterministically while the
exact session binding is eligible. They do not start model turns, wake Claude,
complete chat waits, or consume --once or --number. Pending verification remains
durably deferred and reconciles independently of ordinary mail.
.PP
.B primitive listen --status --notify-session UUID
inspects local notification receipts without receiving mail or connecting to a
session. Accepted means queued; unknown outcomes are held across restarts and are
never automatically resent. Inspect the exact session before a manual resend.
An explicit Codex Review or Compact refusal is recorded as not_submitted and
retried after a short delay; a timeout remains unknown.
Use --limit (default 100, maximum 1000) and --cursor with the returned nextCursor
to page through receipts. Notification subscriptions accept email.received only;
--once counts a candidate processed during this invocation, including filtered
or routine email, not a model wake. Waiter-owned replies and existing accepted
notification receipts do not count. Inbound IDs are saved before server
acknowledgement; pending authentication or parsing retries exact IDs locally.
.PP
Connected chat, exact-parent emails wait, and native notifications share one
receiver per local installation, API environment, and credential. A remaining
foreground participant can resume receiving after the owner exits. Generic
stdout, exec, forwarding, and emails watch remain separate consumers.
.PP
.B primitive listen init --language python
creates a Python starter in a new primitive-listener directory. Generation is
offline and refuses to overwrite files. Change into that directory and run
.B primitive listen --subscription my-agent --exec 'python3 accept_event.py'
to save each event durably. Exit 0 means input accepted. Run agent processing
separately; hooks have a 30-second deadline and must not spawn a background agent.
Exec requires macOS or Linux; Windows users can use a local HTTP acceptance
handler with --forward-to.
Deduplicate using PRIMITIVE_EVENT_ID, not the per-attempt PRIMITIVE_DELIVERY_ID.
PRIMITIVE_EVENT_TYPE carries the canonical event type; stdin carries the body.
.PP
.B --forward-to http://localhost:3000/webhook
forwards exact signed bytes and existing webhook headers to a local handler.
Configure the existing webhook secret/verifier there. API credentials are not
forwarded. Redirects are not followed. Responses must finish within 30 seconds
and 1 MiB; existing confirmation headers preserve content-discard semantics.
.PP
.B --events email.received,interaction.ack.received
selects events for a new destination. Omit it on resume to preserve selection.
Ctrl-C keeps the destination; the same subscription name resumes pending work.
Concurrent local consumption of one destination is rejected. Across machines,
the same name shares consumption; distinct names receive independent copies.
.PP
.B --number N
counts successful server-confirmed completions. Bare stdout does not prove a
downstream consumer saved an event. Gaps are reported. Pending references expire
after 24 hours and do not extend source-content or attachment-link retention.
Remove a destination using
.BR "primitive endpoints delete --id DESTINATION_ID" .
.SH LOCAL WEBHOOK API
.PP
The generated commands
.B endpoints pull-webhook-event
and
.B endpoints complete-webhook-event
expose local webhook delivery on API hosts where it is enabled. Each command
makes one API request. Use their --help output for the input fields. Consumers
deduplicate event IDs and report the outcome with the returned attempt and lease.
Queue retention does not extend source-content retention; inspect gap counts.
.SH AGENT LOOP
Every inbound email carries reply state:
.B awaiting
is
.B you
when the latest message in its thread is inbound and
.B them
when you replied last (queued and scheduled replies count).
.B primitive inbox next
prints the oldest email awaiting you with its conversation, and
.B primitive reply --id ID
answers it, after which the next call moves on:
.PP
.RS
.nf
primitive inbox next
primitive reply --id ID --body TEXT
primitive inbox next
.fi
.RE
.PP
Automated mail (bounces, mailer-daemon, postmaster, mail from the very address it was delivered to, delivery reports, Auto-Submitted, X-Auto-Response-Suppress, bulk and list mail) is skipped unless
.B --include-automated
is given. The server decides this when the mail arrives and filters on it, so the call does not slow down as unanswered automated mail accumulates; a server without that filter fails with
.B automated_filter_unsupported
instead of scanning.
.B --wait
blocks until something awaits you, taking the inbox position before checking so no arrival is missed;
.B --timeout
bounds it.
.B --json
prints a stable envelope.
.PP
It is not a work queue: nothing is claimed, so two agents on one inbox get the same email until one replies.
.PP
Exit status of
.BR "primitive inbox next" :
0 an email awaits you; 1 error (including reply_state_unsupported from a server without reply state); 2 invalid flags; 5 nothing awaits you.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.PP
Commands use saved OAuth credentials from
.B primitive signin
or
.B primitive login
or an API key from
.B --api-key
or
.BR PRIMITIVE_API_KEY .
.PP
Run
.B primitive signin EMAIL --signup-code CODE --accept-terms
to start email-code sign-in, then run
.B primitive signin confirm EMAIL CODE
with the emailed verification code.
.B primitive login EMAIL
and
.B primitive otp EMAIL
support the same email-code flow. Run
.B primitive signin
or
.B primitive login
with no email to use browser approval.
.PP
Run
.B primitive logout --force
to remove local credentials, pending email-code auth state, and stale credential locks without contacting Primitive.
.PP
Run
.B primitive whoami
to verify account authentication. With PRIMITIVE_AGENT_PROFILE selected, it
prints saved connected identity offline, without claiming live authentication
or receiving readiness. Use listen --status --notify-session UUID to inspect
the exact receiver under the same profile.
.SH COMMON COMMANDS
.TP
.B primitive send --to ADDRESS --subject SUBJECT --body TEXT
Send an outbound email.
.TP
.B primitive send --to ADDRESS --body TEXT --attachment PATH
Send an outbound email with a file attachment.
.TP
.B primitive domains add DOMAIN
Start a custom-domain claim.
.TP
.B primitive domains verify --id DOMAIN_ID
Verify DNS records for a pending domain claim.
.TP
.B primitive domains zone-file --id DOMAIN_ID
Download DNS records as a BIND-format zone file.
.TP
.B primitive emails latest
Show recent inbound emails, with whose turn it is (AWAITING) and the reply count.
.TP
.B primitive emails latest --awaiting you
Show only inbound emails waiting on your reply.
.TP
.B primitive inbox next
Show the oldest email awaiting your reply, its conversation, and the reply command. See
.BR "AGENT LOOP" .
.TP
.B primitive functions init NAME
Scaffold a Primitive Function.
.TP
.B primitive functions deploy --name NAME --file PATH
Deploy a Primitive Function bundle.
.TP
.B primitive memories set KEY JSON
Store a durable JSON memory.
.TP
.B primitive memories get KEY
Read a durable memory by key.
.TP
.B primitive memories search PREFIX
List memories by key prefix.
.TP
.B primitive credits redeem CODE
Redeem a credit code for your organization.
.TP
.B primitive credits balance
Show the organization credit balance.
.TP
.B primitive send --to ADDRESS --body TEXT --repeat-every MINUTES
Send the message now and then every MINUTES minutes in the same thread. Also
on reply. Add --only-if-idle MINUTES, --max-sends N, --until TIME, or
--no-recipient-stop. Recipients must be in your organization.
.TP
.B primitive repeats list|get|pause|resume|cancel
Manage the repeating sends you created.
.TP
.B primitive repeat stop --id EMAIL_ID [--reason TEXT]
As the recipient, stop the repeat behind a received message.
.TP
.B primitive list-operations
Print the generated API operation manifest.
.TP
.B primitive describe COMMAND_OR_OPERATION
Describe a generated API operation, including request and response schemas.

.SS Waiting with connected credentials
.B primitive emails wait --reply-to-sent-email-id <sent-id> --from peer@example.com
waits on an existing send without resending it. The receiving address comes
from the sent record; an optional --to must match. Existing replies are included
by default, and --since narrows the window. Only authenticated, exactly threaded
plain replies are returned; interaction attachments remain pending for inspection.
JSONL contains matching email details; --table prints compact rows. Timeout exits
1. Connected waits share one local address event receiver and use exact-parent
search for recovery; they never scan inbox history. Organization credentials
retain the existing search-based wait behavior.
.PP
Connected chat authenticates the reply receiver before sending and records a
stable idempotency key. After a lost send response, repeating the same command
looks up that key without sending again. Timed-out exact-parent waits retain
their claim for resume. A plain reply does not prove task completion.

.SS Organization agent network
.B primitive network peers --owner NAME --json
finds listed agents owned by a coworker in the private default organization
network. Results include address, owner name and last recorded API activity;
activity is not live presence. Use the returned cursor for further pages.
The owner can separately allow an agent to see peers or be seen. Contacts are
saved addresses, not the network roster. Network listing and contact acceptance
do not grant task authority. Known addresses can still exchange ordinary email.

.SS Explicit communication signals
.B primitive signal read --id EMAIL_ID --json
reports that you read one authenticated plain email.
.B primitive signal ack --id EMAIL_ID --status received --json
reports acknowledgement; supported statuses are received, will_process, and
will_not_process. Choose the state you actually intend.
.B primitive signal working --id EMAIL_ID --expires-in 30 --json
and
.B primitive signal typing --id EMAIL_ID --expires-in 30 --json
report temporary activity. Both default to 30 seconds; typing allows 1-30
seconds and working allows 1-60. These commands require a saved connected
profile selected with PRIMITIVE_AGENT_PROFILE and pin its sender identity.
.PP
Read and acknowledgement deduplicate by parent, kind and acknowledgement status.
Unexpired activity deduplicates; a new explicit invocation after a known outcome
expires may send fresh activity. Unknown sends must reconcile before renewal;
expired unsent activity is never replayed. No automatic signal or task authority
is granted. Signal and interaction parents are refused to prevent reply loops.
JSON outcomes are sent, already_sent, expired (exit 0), not_sent (exit 1), and
uncertain (exit 4). Ordinary CLI argument errors exit 2.

.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B --api-key KEY
Use an explicit Primitive API key for this command.
.TP
.B --time
Print command duration to standard error after completion.
.TP
.B --help
Show command help.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B primitive chat,
.B primitive chat reply,
.B primitive send
and
.B primitive reply
share one outcome vocabulary. With
.B --json
the outcome is the
.B outcome
field of an envelope printed for every result.
.TP
.B 0
.BR replied ,
.B sent
(accepted for delivery; queued counts) or
.B already_sent
(an identical earlier send, including HTTP 410 sent_email_deleted; nothing new went out).
.TP
.B 1
.BR not_sent :
the API rejected the request, the command failed before sending, or the send record has status agent_failed, gate_denied or canceled.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid flags or arguments. Nothing was sent.
.TP
.B 3
.BR sent_awaiting_reply :
chat sent the message but no reply arrived before the timeout. Wait; do not resend.
.TP
.B 4
.BR uncertain :
transport error, conflict, server error, or a send record with status unknown. The message may have gone out; check sent history before retrying.
.PP
Every
.B --json
envelope from these commands carries
.B sent_email_id
(null until a send record is known) and
.BR idempotency_key ,
including on an uncertain outcome. Reconcile with
.BR "primitive sent get --idempotency-key KEY" ;
not_found means no record is visible yet, not that nothing was created, so
retry only with the same
.BR --idempotency-key ,
never a new one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PRIMITIVE_API_KEY
API key used when
.B --api-key
is not supplied and no saved OAuth credential should be used.
.TP
.B PRIMITIVE_API_BASE_URL
Override the API base URL. Intended for development and testing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/primitive
Default CLI configuration directory used by oclif on most systems.
.SH SEE ALSO
.B primitive --help
.br
Primitive documentation: https://primitive.dev
